course: dictionary
lesson: 9525
title: Stinging: one word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. Stinging is the -ing form of sting, and it can describe a thing that hurts with a sting, a sharp burning pain, or words that are very hurtful. Look at the words around it to find which meaning is in use.
explain: «Box jellyfish have tentacles covered in tiny stinging cells.» Here stinging describes the cells, which hurt by firing into skin. «A nettle grows stinging hairs.» The hairs are what hurt you, so the nettle is a stinging plant.
explain: Stinging can also describe pain or words. A stinging pain is sharp and burning, and stinging words are very hurtful. Try your meaning in the sentence: if the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right one.
fact: Stinging is the -ing form of the verb sting, and it can work as an adjective that describes a noun.
fact: «Stinging nettles» are plants whose hairs can hurt your skin.
fact: «Stinging pain» is a sharp, burning pain.
fact: «Stinging words» are very hurtful words.
fact: Box jellyfish have stinging cells, but comb jellies are not true jellyfish and have no stinging cells.
exercise: «Box jellyfish have tentacles covered in tiny stinging cells.» What does stinging mean here? | *Able to hurt with a sting | Loud and noisy | Shiny and bright | Very slow
exercise: Leo says, «I felt a stinging pain in my knee.» What kind of pain is it? | Cold and wet | *Sharp and burning | Dull and heavy, like a sleepy ache | Happy and warm
exercise: «Her stinging words made him cry.» What do stinging words mean? | Very kind | *Very hurtful | Very funny | Very quiet
test: In «a nettle grows stinging hairs», which word tells you the hairs can hurt? | nettle | hairs | grows | *stinging
test: «Comb jellies have rows of tiny beating hairs.» Do comb jellies have stinging cells? | *No, they have no stinging cells | Only the young ones do | Yes, they sting like bees | Yes, they fire harpoons
exam: Which meaning fits best in «a stinging cold on the hill»? | Slow and tired | *Sharp and biting, so cold it hurts | Soft and sleepy | Happy and bright
